    "result_text": "All tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: household-capped membership level\n\nThe spec required that a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their existing household members, returning the capped value (no cap when the household has no existing member).\n\nThe `membershipLevel` was previously a purely derived field computed on read from membership points. Since the cap depends on the household's state *at creation time*, I made the level a persisted, create-time-fixed field (like the other derived-at-create fields such as `namesakeCount` and `membershipNumber`).\n\n**Files changed:**\n\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `membershipLevel` field (`membership_level` column) with getter/setter.\n- **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `membershipLevel(Owner)` now returns the stored capped value when present, falling back to the points-derived level otherwise (keeps older/unstamped owners working).\n- **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `assignMembershipLevel(owner)` to the create pipeline (after the other derived fields, since the level depends on the namesake count): it computes the points-derived level, then caps it at `min(natural, maxHouseholdMemberLevel + 1)` when household members exist.\n- **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `membership_level` column (tests run against `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema had to be updated).\n- **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 documented the cap on `membershipLevel`.\n\n**One required behavioral change to note:** the new test creates two owners in the same household (same last name + postcode) without `sharesHousehold` and expects both to succeed. The existing hard \"household duplicate\" block rejected the second with a 409, which is fundamentally incompatible with the spec's premise of a new owner having \"household members.\" I therefore relaxed `applyHouseholdMembership` so owners may join an existing household (the newcomer's level is capped instead of the create being rejected), and updated the `sharesHousehold`/OpenAPI docs to match \u2014 `sharesHousehold` now only governs the `possibleDuplicate` soft flag. Hard identity duplicates (same telephone/email/household) are still rejected by the separate identity-key check.\n\nThe audit log confirms the behavior: the first household member got `membershipLevel=1`, the second's natural level of 2 was allowed (capped at `1 + 1 = 2`).",
